easing  tension as this evacuation  operation winds down it's been very  quiet certainly the last i'd say five or  six hours we've heard  no planes taking off or landing at the  airport certainly from here  and we've heard obviously hundreds of  aircraft  in and around the airport overhead in  recent days we understand  that the  the process was still going on this  morning because we spoke to a group of  people on a bus who told us that they  had all the  requisite documents the either a visa or  a passport or some sort of  official invitation and they've been had  their papers processed and been told to  head to the airport on this bus and then  had been turned away  we know the taliban security has uh been  stepped up  in recent days because of this threat  level of terror attacks and certainly in  the wake of those horrific  that horrific attack on thursday that  killed at least 110 people including  13 u.s servicemen  so  the situation is unclear about what  exactly has happened this evening but  the situation we do know is very tense  and it's unclear as to whether the  evacuations are indeed over  yet  or whether there are still people that  will get into the airport and get on to  flights  all right that was charles stratford  from kabul let's continue this now  u.s president joe biden has been taking  part in a ceremony to bring home the  bodies of service personnel killed in  kabul he's received the bodies of 13  people they were among the victims  killed by suicide bombers at the airport  on thursday isil k claimed  responsibility the u.s targeted the  group with drone strikes in nangarhar  province  and the secretary of state anthony  blinken has addressed the media he  maintains the us will continue to have  the ability to attack targets in  afghanistan even without soldiers on the  ground  we have the capacity  around the world including in  afghanistan  to  take uh to find and to take strikes  against terrorists who uh who want to do  us harm and as you know in uh country  after country including places like uh  like yemen uh like somalia uh large  parts of syria libya places where we  don't have boots on the ground on any  kind of  ongoing basis  we have the capacity to go after people  who are trying to do us harm we'll  retain that capacity in afghanistan  let's bring in now rob reynolds he's  standing by live for us in washington dc  and rob so what are officials saying  about the result of that u.s drone  strike in afghanistan  well in a statement that charlie  referred to from the u.s central command  the  military says that they successfully  carried out a drone strike against a  vehicle  they said that they  observed secondary explosions after  hitting the vehicle  which they  said  indicated that it had  on board a large amount of bomb or  explosive material  so  they are saying that this was an isil k  operation that they disrupted uh they uh  said in a uh in in their statement that  there was no indication of civilian  casualties so how that squares with what  uh charlie and others are seeing on the  ground with reports of civilian  casualties is not known but the u.s  military says it is going to continue uh  to do uh this sort of post-operation  assessment of what's going on there and  uh very interesting sami uh uh that's  the sound bite from the u.s secretary of  state anthony blinken that he played a  moment ago indicating that this sort of  thing this uh  air strikes drone strikes over the  horizon as they're called  attacks  will continue in afghanistan so the  actual  shooting war the fighting war that's  that has been going on for two decades  may be  drawing to a conclusion but the uh the  the actual the the the fighting and the  the warfare the acts of war may be  continuing for some time to come how  long who knows  and rob is an understanding coming  together for  evacuations of americans perhaps others  after august the 31st  yes uh well at least the u.s national  security adviser jake sullivan  said today and there was a release of a  statement from the state department as  well saying that 97 countries had banded  together with the united states to  demand that the  afghan  taliban honor commitments  to allow safe passage for foreign  nationals  and  afghans who possess  the necessary transfer papers to  relocate to  third countries and allow them safe  passage out of the country so of course  if the airport is closing down with the  departure of u.s troops uh and uncertain  how how many flights will go in and out  or who will actually control that  airstrip that leaves the ground routes  outside of afghanistan as a possible  exit plan for these people there are  about 250  u.s citizens still remaining in the  country and of course many thousands of  afghans associated with the nato effort  who would also like to leave  all right we'll leave you there thanks  so much rob reynolds
 

